                2026-11-17 17:04:03 UTC (releng/14.4, 14.4-RELEASE-p13)
CVE Name:       CVE-2026-14456, CVE-2026-35189, CVE-2026-35191,
                CVE-2026-42772, CVE-2026-54872, CVE-2026-54873,
                CVE-2026-54875, CVE-2026-72897, CVE-2026-75803,
                CVE-2026-75804, CVE-2026-75805, CVE-2026-75806,
                CVE-2026-77696, CVE-2026-84784

For general information regarding FreeBSD Errata Notices and Security
Advisories, including descriptions of the fields above, security
branches, and the following sections, please visit
<URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

FreeBSD includes software from the OpenSSL Project.  The OpenSSL Project is a
collaborative effort to develop a robust, commercial-grade, full-featured
Open Source toolkit for the Transport Layer Security (TLS) protocol.  It is
also a general-purpose cryptography library.

II.  Problem Description

Recent OpenSSL security advisories were addressed on the release branches by
patching only the most severe issues.  The remaining lower severity fixes
from OpenSSL 3.0.21 and 3.0.22 (FreeBSD 14) and 3.5.8 and 3.5.9 (FreeBSD
15) were not applied.  This update imports the current upstream point
release on each branch.

The import resolves several issues affecting different OpenSSL versions,
and therefore different FreeBSD versions, along with some fixes that have
no CVE assigned.  Instead of listing detailed writeups for each issue,
please see the referenced advisories from OpenSSL.

Issues affecting FreeBSD 15.1 (OpenSSL 3.5):
  CVE-2026-14456 - Unbounded memory growth in the QUIC server channel queue
  CVE-2026-35189 - Excessive memory allocation in relative CRLDP processing
  CVE-2026-35191 - QUIC amplification credit may be over-accounted
  CVE-2026-42772 - Quadratic-time fragment reassembly in QUIC
  CVE-2026-54872 - Timing side-channel in non-NIST EC scalar multiplication
  CVE-2026-54873 - Denial of Service via QUIC STREAM fragment metadata
  CVE-2026-54875 - Non-constant-time SM2 scalar multiply on arm64 and RISC-V
  CVE-2026-72897 - Out-of-bounds access after mid-handshake SSL_set_SSL_CTX()
  CVE-2026-75803 - AEAD forgery with empty ciphertext via EVP_Cipher()
  CVE-2026-75804 - QUIC connection-level flow control not enforced
  CVE-2026-75805 - NULL dereference handling CMP client revocation responses
  CVE-2026-75806 - Denial of Service via undersized DTLS 1.2 AEAD records
  CVE-2026-77696 - Timing side-channel in SM2 signature generation
  CVE-2026-84784 - Unbounded QUIC RETIRE_CONNECTION_ID backlog

Issues affecting FreeBSD 14.4 and 14.5 (OpenSSL 3.0):
  CVE-2026-75803 - AEAD forgery with empty ciphertext via EVP_Cipher()

III. Impact

The issues include an out-of-bounds memory access, a NULL pointer
dereference, an AEAD tag forgery, timing side-channels that may leak private
key material, and several paths that allow a remote peer to force excessive
memory or CPU use.  Most result in a Denial of Service (DoS).  See the
OpenSSL advisories for specific details.

Automated vulnerability scanners that key on the OpenSSL version also report
the affected releases as vulnerable to issues that were already patched.

IV.  Workaround

No workaround is available.

V.   Solution

Upgrade your system to a supported FreeBSD stable or release / security
branch (releng) dated after the correction date.  A reboot is required
following the upgrade to ensure that all applications and kernel code are
rebuilt with the updated OpenSSL-provided code.

Perform one of the following:

1) To update your system installed from base system packages:

Systems running a 15.0-RELEASE or later version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or
arm64 platforms, which were installed using base system packages, can be
updated via the pkg(8) utility:

# pkg upgrade -r FreeBSD-base
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for an erratum fix"

2) To update your system installed from binary distribution sets:

Systems running a RELEASE version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or arm64 platforms
which were not installed using base system packages can be updated via the
freebsd-update(8) utility:

# freebsd-update fetch
# freebsd-update install
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for an erratum fix"

3) To update your system via a source code patch:

The following patches have been verified to apply to the applicable
FreeBSD release branches.

a) Download the relevant patch from the location below, and verify the
detached PGP signature using your PGP utility.

[FreeBSD 15.1]
Due to the size of the patch, this has been split amongst 5 parts.
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part1.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part1.patch.asc
# gpg --verify openssl-15.part1.patch.asc
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part2.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part2.patch.asc
# gpg --verify openssl-15.part2.patch.asc
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part3.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part3.patch.asc
# gpg --verify openssl-15.part3.patch.asc
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part4.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part4.patch.asc
# gpg --verify openssl-15.part4.patch.asc
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part5.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-15.part5.patch.asc
# gpg --verify openssl-15.part5.patch.asc

[FreeBSD 14.5]
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-14.5.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-14.5.patch.asc
# gpg --verify openssl-14.5.patch.asc

[FreeBSD 14.4]
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-14.4.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:31/openssl-14.4.patch.asc
# gpg --verify openssl-14.4.patch.asc

b) Apply the patch.  Execute the following commands as root:

# cd /usr/src
# patch -E -p0 < /path/to/patch

c) Recompile the operating system using buildworld and installworld as
described in <URL:https://www.FreeBSD.org/handbook/makeworld.html>
and reboot the system.

VI.  Correction details

This issue is corrected as of the corresponding Git commit hash in the
following stable and release branches:

Branch/path                             Hash                     Revision
